<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Racial segregation</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Racial segregation is the enforced separation of people of different races in housing, public life and rights. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> codified racial segregation in its African colonies through a series of laws, particularly after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. These measures were designed to preserve Italian dominance over the colonised populations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/racial-segregation.jpg" alt="Racial segregation" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esther Bubley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1943_Colored_Waiting_Room_Sign.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Racial segregation is the separation of people into racial or other ethnic groups in daily life. It can mean spatial separation and the compulsory use of different institutions, such as schools and hospitals, and it may apply to restaurants, buses, housing, hotels and places of worship. Segregation often allows close contact between members of different groups in hierarchical situations, for example when a person of one race works as a servant for a member of another. Involuntary segregation was often used to secure the advantages of a dominant group, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, the Bantu and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Before modern concepts of race developed, some states enforced separation on grounds of ethnicity or religion. In late antiquity <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> restricted some marriages between provincials and barbarian soldiers. Legal distinctions between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in Visigothic Spain lasted until 654, and in 1366 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statutes-of-kilkenny">Statutes of Kilkenny</a> prohibited marriage and other close relations between the English and the Irish. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> an edict of 779 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> to wear their ethnic dress and banned them from marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> women, and in 836 the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>, Lu Chun, banned intermarriage and made it illegal for foreigners to own property.</p><p>In 1204 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to separate themselves from Christians and to wear distinctive clothing, and forced segregation spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 14th and 15th centuries. In the Russian Empire Jews were confined to the Pale of Settlement. From the early 15th century Jews in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> were confined to walled quarters called mellahs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Racial segregation has generally been outlawed worldwide, and can amount to the international crime of apartheid and a crime against humanity under the 2002 Rome Declaration of Statute of the International Criminal Court. In French Algeria a colonial law of 1865 allowed Arab and Berber Algerians to apply for French citizenship only if they gave up their Muslim identity. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> segregation was embedded in society before the constitution of 1982, and the Supreme Court upheld it in 1939. The last school segregated for Black pupils in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> closed in 1965, the last in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> in 1983, and the last racially segregated Indigenous school, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, in 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Racial segregation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The administration introduced <b>racial segregation</b>, codified from 1937 in laws forbidding marriage and concubinage between Italians and Africans, to preserve Italian dominance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of South Africa moved from the most elaborate system of legal <b>racial segregation</b> of the twentieth century to a negotiated multiracial democracy in 1994, and as one of Africa’s largest economies and a leading voice of the Global South it remains central to the continent’s politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Racial segregation</b> in South Africa long predated apartheid.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States the doctrine of states’ rights was invoked to defend slavery and then the <b>racial segregation</b> of the southern states, and federal troops had to enforce the Supreme Court’s ruling against segregated schools in Brown v. Board of Education of 1954, as at Little Rock,…</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>…come to power in 1948 and governed for the next 46 years; under apartheid, a brutal system of <b>racial segregation</b> whose name meant &quot;separateness&quot; in Afrikaans, the white minority held all political power and black South Africans were forced to live in poverty-stricken townships and denied basic human…</blockquote><a href="/songs/meadowlands">Meadowlands</a></li><li><blockquote>The US Supreme Court ruled that <b>racial segregation</b> on Louisiana railways did not violate the Constitution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
